This access pattern is fragile. `payload.review && payload.review.author_association` evaluates to the string value of `author_association` when it exists — which is fine. However, if `author_association` is an empty string (which GitHub can return for deleted accounts), this would evaluate to `''` (falsy), and `authorAllowed` would receive `undefined` instead of the empty string, potentially changing the gating logic.

Consider destructuring up front like the other branches do:
```js
const reviewAuthorAssoc = (payload.review && payload.review.author_association) || '';
// ...
if (hasCommand(commentBody) && authorAllowed(reviewAuthorAssoc, commenter)) {
```
This makes the fallback explicit and consistent with how `issue_comment` handles `payload.comment.author_association` (which is never null/undefined in practice, but the defensive pattern is still better).

